# =============================================================================
# geometry/singleice/singleice_2D_L1mm_R250um_keff.opts — curved-interface benchmark
# for k_eff homogenization (-keff). NOT a physics run.
#
# One ice disk of radius 250 um centred in a 1 mm periodic cell: a square array
# of disks, ice fraction f = pi R^2 / L^2 = 0.19635. Its sharp effective
# conductivity is known to far better than the tolerances used on it
# (Rayleigh 1892; Perrins, McKenzie & McPhedran 1979):
#
#   k_00 = k_11 = 0.0295684 W/m/K      (Maxwell-Garnett alone: 0.0295632)
#   k_01 = k_10 = 0                     (square symmetry)
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE THE LAMINATE. The laminate's interface normal is
# exactly the y axis, so under -keff_interp tensor the conductivity tensor is
# diagonal everywhere and its off-diagonal terms are never exercised. Here the
# normal sweeps every direction, so an error in the n (x) n assembly shows up
# directly in k_00. It is also the geometry where the arithmetic law's bias is
# large: a conducting disk in an insulating matrix sends its flux NORMAL to the
# interface, which is the direction the arithmetic law gets wrong. First-order
# prediction (dipole field, no fitted constants): +30% at eps = L/50, +3.8% at
# L/400. See studies/keff_sharp_limit/disk/keff_disk_analytic.py.
#
# Run the ladder through the committed driver, which scales Ny with eps:
#
#   ./studies/keff_sharp_limit/disk/verify_keff_disk.sh
# =============================================================================

-ic_type single_ice
-dim 2

-Lx 1.0e-3
-Ly 1.0e-3
-Nx 256
-Ny 256

-p 2
-C 1
-periodic 1                       # REQUIRED by -keff

-RCice 2.5e-4

-eps 2.0e-5                       # same reference point as the laminate: eps/dy = 5.12
-eps_temp_override 1              # eps here is chosen for resolution, not from comp_eps.py

-delt_t 1.0e-4
